A ﬁrst regularization technique consists in adding a regularization term on the weights to the loss function L. The idea is that one does not know in advance the complexity of the model one wants to approximate trough the neural network. By default, one chooses a model with a high complexity i. e. with a high number of parameters. However, the use of too many parameters ampliﬁes the overﬁtting phenomenon. Intuitively, one would like to add a penalization on the number of parameters used in the model; but the 0-norm is not diﬀerentiable and cannot be used. A way to counteract this issue is to use the L1 and L2 regularizations [8], in order to introduce sparsity, with some weights tending to 0. This way, it gets a similar impact as controlling the number of parameters of the network, while keeping a diﬀerentiable cost function.
